        "projectInstructionsLoaded": [],
        "finalMessage": "I changed the clickable header in `collapsible.tsx` from a `<div>` to a real button, which should clear all three warnings at line 122. I couldn't confirm that: I didn't run the linter because the command to find it needed approval.\n\n**What changed**\n- The header row is now `<button type=\"button\">` instead of a `<div>`. A button is already focusable and responds to Enter and Space. That should satisfy all three rules: the element is now interactive, so it no longer counts as static or non-interactive, and it already has keyboard support.\n- I added `aria-expanded={open}`, so screen readers announce whether the section is open or closed.\n- To keep it looking the same, I reset the browser's default button styles: no background or border, inherited color and font, no padding, and full width (a `<div>` fills the row by default; a button doesn't). The existing flex layout and `cursor: \"pointer\"` are unchanged.\n- I left `onClick={openTrigger}`, `className` and the children as they were, so clicking still toggles the section.\n\n**Things to know**\n- I didn't use `role=\"button\"` with `tabIndex` and a key handler on the `<div>`. It's more code, and Ultracite's `useSemanticElements` rule would likely flag it and say to use a `<button>`.\n- Where this component is used, the label is an `<h3>`, so the heading now sits inside a `<button>`. Browsers render this fine, but it isn't valid HTML. Screen readers also treat everything inside a button as plain text, so the heading no longer appears in their heading lists. The fully correct pattern is `<h3><button aria-expanded>…</button></h3>`. That means changing how the label is passed in from `Stats/index.tsx`, which is outside the one file I was allowed to edit.",
